ent","content":"<a name=\"section-unemployment\"></a><h2>Unemployment rate rose in in South Somerset compared with the previous year</h2><p>Around 4,400 people in South Somerset were unemployed in the year ending March 2023. This is a rate of 5.2%.</p><p>This was an increase compared with the year ending  March 2022 when the unemployment rate was 2.1%.</p><p>Across the South West, from the year ending  March 2022 to the year ending March 2023, there was a decrease in the unemployment rate from 2.8% to 2.7%.</p><p>Year on year, the number of people unemployed in the South West fell from around 79,500 to around 77,500 over the same period.</p><p>Unemployment across Great Britain dropped between the year ending March 2022 and the year ending  March 2023, from around 1,350,000 people (3.6%) to around 1,200,000 (3.6%) in the year ending March 2022.</p><p>Unemployed people are those without jobs who are actively seeking work and available to take up a job. Some jobless people may not seek work or be able to work for various reasons, such as retirement, sickness, or study, and are called \"economically inactive\". </p><p>This means that the employment rate and the unemployment rate can both go up at the same time as people move into the labour market and become job seekers.</p><p>The unemployment rate is the number of unemployed people out of everyone  \"economically active\" in the labour market, that is, everyone who is employed or looking for work. The employment rate is out of the working-age population including  \"economically inactive\" people. </p>"},{"id":"chart-highest","type":"Chart","chartType":"bar","title":"Highest unemployment rates (%) in the South West, year ending March 2023","footer":"Source: Model-based estimates of unemployment from the Office for National Statistics","data":[{"x":6.8,"y":"Sedgemoor"},{"x":6.3,"y":"Mid Devon"},{"x":5.2,"y":"South Somerset"},{"x":4.9,"y":"Forest of Dean"},{"x":4.6,"y":"Swindon"}],"padding-left":"160"},{"type":"end","content":"<p><a href=\"#section-top\">Back to top</a></p>"},{"type":"ClaimantCount","content":"<a name=\"section-claimant\"></a><h2>Claimant Count in South Somerset was around the same as a year earlier</h2><p>Claimant Count is an experimental statistic that measures the number of people who are <a href=\"https://www.ons.gov.uk/employmentandlabourmarket/peopleinwork/employmentandemployeetypes/methodologies/aguidetolabourmarketstatistics#claimant-count\">claiming unemployment-related benefits.</a></p><p>The Claimant Count in South Somerset was 698.3 in the year ending July 2023, around the same as  the year ending July 2022, when there were 698.3 claimants. </p><p>The proportion of people in South Somerset aged 16 to 64 years in South Somerset who were claiming unemployment-related benefits was 2.1% in the year ending July 2023, very similar compared with the year ending July 2022 when it was 2.1%.</p>"},{"id":"chart-claimanttime","type":"Chart","chartType":"line-highlight","title":"Claimant count (%) in the South West, July 2012 to July 2023","footer":"Source: Claimant Count from the Office for National Statistics","data":[{"z":"Bath and North East Somerset","x":"2013","y":1.7},{"z":"Bath and North East Somerset","x":"2014","y":1.2},{"z":"Bath and North East Somerset","x":"2015","y":1},{"z":"Bath and North East Somerset","x":"2016","y":0.9},{"z":"Bath and North East Somerset","x":"2017","y":1.6},{"z":"Bath and North East Somerset","x":"2018","y":1.7},{"z":"Bath and North East Somerset","x":"2019","y":1.8},{"z":"Bath and North East Somerset","x":"2020","y":4.1},{"z":"Bath and North East Somerset","x":"2021","y":3.2},{"z":"Bath and North East Somerset","x":"2022","y":1.8},{"z":"Bath and North East Somerset","x":"2023","y":1.8},{"z":"Bristol, City of","x":"2013","y":3.6},{"z":"Bristol, City of","x":"2014","y":2.5},{"z":"Bristol, City of","x":"2015","y":1.8},{"z":"Bristol, City of","x":"2016","y":1.6},{"z":"Bristol, City of","x":"2017","y":1.7},{"z":"Bristol, City of","x":