:1.7},{"z":"Gloucester","x":"2017","y":1.5},{"z":"Gloucester","x":"2018","y":2},{"z":"Gloucester","x":"2019","y":2.7},{"z":"Gloucester","x":"2020","y":6},{"z":"Gloucester","x":"2021","y":4.8},{"z":"Gloucester","x":"2022","y":3.1},{"z":"Gloucester","x":"2023","y":3.3}],"selected":"South Somerset","padding-right":"90"},{"type":"end","content":"<p><a href=\"#section-top\">Back to top</a></p>"},{"type":"Inactivity","content":"<a name=\"section-inactive\"></a><h2>Economic inactivity has decreased in South Somerset since the previous year</h2><p>Around 17,300  people or 18.2% of the population aged 16 to 64 years in South Somerset were \"economically inactive\" in the year ending March 2023.</p><p>People are classed as \"economically inactive\" if they are not in employment but don't meet the criteria for being \"unemployed\". This means they have not been seeking work within the previous four weeks or were unable to start work within the next two weeks. Common reasons include being retired, looking after the home or family or being temporarily or long-term sick and disabled.</p><p>Economic inactivity in South Somerset is lower than across the South West and Great Britain.</p><p>Across the South West, 19.3% of people aged 16 to 64 years were economically inactive during the year ending  2022, a decrease compared with the same period in March 2022 when 19.8% were economically inactive.</p><p>Overall in Great Britain, 21.6% of people aged 16 to 64 years were economically inactive, compared with 21.5% in the same period of March 2022.</p>"},{"id":"chart-inactivity","type":"Chart","chartType":"bar","title":"Highest economic inactivity rates in the South West, residents aged 16 to 64 years (%), year ending March 2023","footer":"Source: Annual Population Survey from the Office for National Statistics","data":[{"x":27.6,"y":"South Hams"},{"x":24.2,"y":"Exeter"},{"x":24.1,"y":"Plymouth"},{"x":22.8,"y":"Teignbridge"},{"x":22.3,"y":"Torbay"}],"padding-left":"160"},{"type":"end","co